{
  "_README": [
    "THE CROSS-LANGUAGE SUBJECT-CANONICALISATION PARITY CORPUS.",
    "",
    "This file MUST be byte-identical in both repositories:",
    "  kag_common/tests/fixtures/subject-canonicalisation-parity.json",
    "  fr/backend/tests/fixtures/subject-canonicalisation-parity.json",
    "Each repo's attestation test suite asserts (a) that its own implementation maps every",
    "`subject` below to exactly the `canonical` beside it, and (b) that this file's sha256",
    "equals a literal recorded in that suite. The same sha256 literal appears in both",
    "suites, so editing the corpus on one side alone turns red on that side and the",
    "divergence surfaces at review instead of in production.",
    "",
    "WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `_normalise_subject` (Python) and `canonicalSubject`",
    "(TypeScript) are two implementations of ONE contract. Two rounds of fixes were",
    "applied to them independently and both rounds diverged, because each suite tested its",
    "own implementation in isolation: every test on both sides was green while Python's",
    "`str.strip()` removed five code points (U+0085, U+001C, U+001D, U+001E, U+001F) that",
    "JavaScript's `trim()` does not. Measured end to end at `required_approvals: 2`, an",
    "approver pair [alice, U+001C+alice] was `insufficient_approvals` in Python and VALID",
    "in TypeScript, and the same trick re-opened the self-approval gate. A shared frozen",
    "corpus is the only instrument that can see that class of defect, because the property",
    "under test is a relation BETWEEN the two implementations, not a property of either.",
    "",
    "`canonical` is the identity key. An EMPTY string means the subject canonicalises to",
    "nothing and the verifier must REFUSE it (`invalid_approver` / a `None` return), not",
    "count it as a subject that matches nothing.",
    "",
    "Rows are hand-reviewed, not generated from either implementation's output."
  ],
